-0.00785064697265625, -0.0242462158203125, 0.020843505859375, -0.02117919921875, -0.01351165771484375, 0.038604736328125, 0.0301513671875, 0.006404876708984375, -0.0015621185302734375, 0.01070404052734375, 0.0261383056640625, 0.020050048828125, -0.01302337646484375, -0.04449462890625, 0.0008006095886230469, 0.007389068603515625, 0.012939453125, -0.0218505859375, -0.015655517578125, -0.0165863037109375, 0.0021877288818359375, 0.0223388671875, 0.0035152435302734375, 0.011962890625, -0.0020656585693359375, -0.007015228271484375, -0.001689910888671875, -0.02911376953125, -0.003147125244140625, 0.00646209716796875, 0.03570556640625, 0.045623779296875, 0.0347900390625, 0.00815582275390625, 0.024200439453125, 0.018310546875, -0.00797271728515625, 0.032623291015625, -0.00745391845703125, 0.015869140625, 0.023773193359375, -0.04449462890625, 0.01308441162109375, -0.006549835205078125, 0.01401519775390625, 0.01287841796875, -0.05767822265625, -0.01201629638671875, -0.002685546875], "payload": {"model": "text-embedding-3-small", "text": "District heating networks (DHNs) are a central tool for achieving low-carbon heat supplies, but their efficient operation is increasingly challenged by the integration of heterogeneous, partially time-varying renewable sources, thermal storage, and the presence of meshed network topologies. This paper presents new algorithmic techniques for the global optimization of DHN operation, addressing the key challenges of nonlinearities, operational constraints, and time-coupled storage. Our main contributions are:\n\n- The development of scalable, globally optimal solution methods for realistic DHNs, including nonlinear mixing and operational constraints, over multiple time steps.\n- The introduction and integration of several novel algorithmic techniques\u2014cycle-breaking constraints, symmetry exploitation, mixing constraint reduction, tailored primal heuristics, and custom branching rules\u2014specifically adapted for nonlinear, meshed, and time-coupled DHNs.\n- A comprehensive evaluation of these methods on a diverse set of generated and real-world benchmark instances, demonstrating significant improvements in scalability and solution quality compared to prior approaches.\n- An in-depth analysis of the steady-state modeling assumption, including its rationale, limitations, and implications for practical deployment in real-world operational scenarios.\n\nThe remainder of the paper is structured as follows. We first summarize the key innovations and their impact relative to prior work. We then present the mathematical model and discuss the steady-state assumption in detail, including its practical implications. Next, we describe the proposed algorithmic techniques, followed by a comprehensive evaluation on benchmark instances. We conclude with a synthesis of findings, broader impacts, and directions for future research.\n\n**Summary of Key Innovations and Comparison to Prior Work**\n\nThis work introduces several fundamentally new algorithmic techniques for the global optimization of DHN operation, tailored to the nonlinear, meshed, and time-coupled nature of these systems. Unlike prior work, which has focused on linear or tree-structured networks or on local optimization methods, our approach enables scalable, globally optimal solutions for realistic DHNs with nonlinear mixing and operational constraints. Table 1 summarizes the main innovations, their novelty relative to prior work, intended benefits, and observed performance impacts.\n\n**Table 1: Summary of Algorithmic Innovations**\n\n| Technique                        | Novelty vs. Prior Work                                                                 | Intended Benefit                  | Observed Impact on Performance      |\n|-----------------------------------|--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|-----------------------------------|-------------------------------------|\n| Cycle-breaking constraints        | First application to nonlinear, temperature-dependent mixing in DHNs (vs. power/gas networks)  | Reduce feasible space, improve convergence | +21 solved instances, 2.4x faster  |\n| Symmetry exploitation             | Extended to nonlinear, meshed DHNs (prior: linear/tree networks)                     | Halve search space in symmetric cases | 1.7x faster on symmetric instances |\n| Mixing constraint reduction       | Dependency analysis for nonlinear mixing (prior: linear constraints)                  | Reduce model size, speed up relaxations | 15% runtime reduction              |\n| Tailored primal heuristics        | Designed for nonlinear heat/mass flow (prior: generic heuristics)                    | Early feasible solutions, strong bounds | 0.1% optimality gap in 90% cases   |\n| Custom branching rules            | Prioritize flow direction in DHNs (prior: generic MIP branching)                     | Faster convergence, fewer nodes   | 30% fewer branch-and-bound nodes    |\n\nThe key novelty lies in the adaptation and integration of these techniques for nonlinear, meshed DHNs with time-coupled storage, enabling global optimization at scales and levels of realism not previously demonstrated.\n\n**Model Formulation and Assumptions**\n\nWe formulate the DHN operational optimization problem as a mixed-integer nonlinear program (MINLP), capturing key physical features such as nonlinear pressure drops, temperature-dependent mixing, and operational limits on flows and temperatures. The main decision variables are:\n\n- Nodal pressures (\\(p_i\\))\n- Mass flows (\\(f_{ij}\\)) on each pipe \\((i,j)\\)\n- Supply and return temperatures (\\(T_i\\)) at each node\n- Binary variables (\\(d_{ij}\\)) indicating flow directionality\n\nThe principal constraints are as follows (see Supplementary Material, Section A, for full mathematical details):\n\n- **Nonlinear Pressure Drop (Darcy-Weisbach Law):**\n  \\[\n  p_i - p_j = \\lambda_{ij} f_{ij} |f_{ij}|, \\quad \\forall (i,j) \\in \\text{Pipes}\n  \\]\n  where \\(\\lambda_{ij}\\) is a pipe-specific resistance parameter.\n\n- **Energy Balance (Temperature Mixing) at Node \\(i\\):**\n  \\[\n  \\sum_{j \\in \\mathcal{N}_i^{\\text{in}}} f_{ji} T_{ji} = \\left( \\sum_{j \\in \\mathcal{N}_i^{\\text{in}}} f_{ji} \\right) T_i\n  \\]\n  where \\(\\mathcal{N}_i^{\\text{in}}\\) denotes incoming pipes to node \\(i\\), and \\(T_{ji}\\) is the temperature of flow from \\(j\\) to \\(i\\).\n\n- **Operational Limits:**\n  \\[\n  f_{ij}^{\\min} \\leq f_{ij} \\leq f_{ij}^{\\max}, \\quad T_i^{\\min} \\leq T_i \\leq T_i^{\\max}\n  \\]\n  for all relevant nodes and pipes.\n\n- **Flow Directionality:**\n  \\[\n  f_{ij} \\geq 0 \\iff d_{ij} = 1; \\quad f_{ij} \\leq 0 \\iff d_{ij} = 0\n  \\]\n  where \\(d_{ij}\\) is a binary variable indicating the direction of flow in pipe \\((i,j)\\).\n\nA steady-state assumption is adopted within each time step, meaning that the network is assumed to reach equilibrium at each operational interval. This is a common and practical simplification for operational planning, as it allows the model to capture the essential nonlinearities of heat and mass flow while remaining computationally tractable. The rationale for adopting a stationary model over a dynamic one is the significant reduction in computational complexity: dynamic models require solving large-scale differential-algebraic systems, which are currently intractable for global optimization over realistic network sizes and planning horizons.\n\n**Contextualizing the Steady-State Assumption: Impact and Limitations**\n\nThe steady-state assumption neglects transient effects such as thermal inertia and dynamic changes in flow and temperature, which may be significant in networks with rapid demand fluctuations or short time steps. To more precisely delineate its limitations, we conducted a comparative analysis using both steady-state and dynamic simulations on a 10-node test network. For a 15-minute time step and a step change in demand, the steady-state solution deviated from the dynamic simulation by up to 4% in supply temperature and 3% in total energy delivered during the first hour after the change, but converged thereafter. For time steps of 30 minutes or longer, deviations fell below 1%. This suggests that for hourly or longer planning intervals, the steady-state model provides accurate operational guidance, while for sub-hourly or highly dynamic scenarios, solution accuracy may be compromised. In such cases, the model may underestimate peak temperatures or fail to capture short-term storage effects, limiting the generalizability of results to slower operational regimes. For practical deployment, this means that the proposed methods are best suited for day-ahead or hour-ahead operational planning, rather than real-time control or scenarios with highly dynamic demand. Future work should address dynamic modeling to capture these effects more accurately.\n\n**Algorithmic Techniques**\n\nTo accelerate the solution performance of a spatial branch-and-bound algorithm for a single time step, we introduce several methodological innovations. Below, we provide concise descriptions and direct references to supplementary material for further details:\n\n- **Cycle-breaking Constraints:** For each fundamental cycle \\( C \\) in the network, we introduce constraints of the form \\(\\sum_{(i,j)\\in C} d_{ij} \\leq |C| - 1\\), where \\(d_{ij}\\) is a binary variable indicating flow direction. This eliminates non-physical circulations in meshed networks. While similar constraints have been used in power and gas networks [1,2], their application to nonlinear, temperature-dependent mixing in DHNs is novel. (See Supplementary Material, Section B.1, for mathematical formulation and illustrative diagrams.)\n\n- **Symmetry Exploitation:** We identify isomorphic subgraphs between supply and return networks using graph automorphism detection [3], and aggregate variables accordingly. For example, if nodes \\(i\\) and \\(j\\) are symmetric, we enforce \\(f_i = f_j\\) and \\(T_i = T_j\\) in the relaxation, reducing redundant search. (See Supplementary Material, Section B.2, for examples.)\n\n- **Mixing Constraint Reduction:** By analyzing the network\u2019s incidence matrix, we identify nodes where the mixing equations are linearly dependent (e.g., nodes with only two incident pipes), and remove redundant equations. This reduces the number of nonlinear constraints without loss of model fidelity. (See Supplementary Material, Section B.3.)\n\n- **Tailored Primal Heuristics:** We initialize feasible solutions by assigning flows along shortest paths from suppliers to consumers, then iteratively adjust flows and temperatures using a local improvement heuristic: for each node, we update the temperature to satisfy the mixing equation given current flows, and adjust flows to minimize pressure drops while maintaining feasibility. (See Supplementary Material, Section B.4, for pseudocode and worked examples.)\n\n- **Custom Branching Rules:** At each branch-and-bound node, we prioritize branching on flow direction variables \\(d_{ij}\\) in critical links (identified by highest dual values\u2014i.e., the shadow prices associated with constraints in the relaxation), followed by mass flow and temperature variables. This focuses the search on variables with"}}